That's because the risk is still too high.  See fountain dipping.

The scroll of wishing is not intended to be a serious suggestion; more
just a way to illustrate that a more ideal state of affairs would be to
allow players who are trying to win to make a reasonable risk-to-reward
assessment.  Right now, the risk so far outweighs the reward for unIDed
scrolls that it's just silly to even bother unless you're about to die
anyway (and even possibly not then, since dust E is 70%).

With a less binary risk-to-reward model, multiple styles of effective
(ie. "winning") gameplay can be developed; right now, all consistently 
winning styles are simple variants on "Don't read unIDed scrolls".
While it works, it could be better.

> Well, maybe reading un-ID scrolls could have slightly different effects  
> than reading ID scroll ("You slowly decipher the words on the scroll", or  
> something similar) and these effects should be more or less unique without  
> being too powerfull (eg enchant armor could enchant several pieces of  
> armor instead of just one, ...)

Adding more random factors doesn't necessarily improve things, and this
either means "add random effects" or "everyone price-IDs scrolls first
and carefully leaves them unidentified."

If stuff gets too random, people will simply be annoyed with it; take, for
example, a possible change to pricing that causes _all_ items to be randomly
base-priced between 50 and 400.  This would add difficulty to the game,
and likely irritate the snot out of players since price-ID being
available is part of what makes Nethack Nethack.

